1
00:00:02.068 --> 00:00:03.303
In this lesson,

2
00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:06.740
you will develop a multi-stage
MongoDB aggregation pipeline.

3
00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:11.711
You will discover how to use metadata
to refine and limit the search results

4
00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:16.745
returned from database operation,
enhancing efficiency and relevancy.

5
00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:20.620
All right, let's have some fun.

6
00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:24.891
Metadata is simply additional information
about a specific data.

7
00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:29.195
It's meant to supplement the key data point
and provide more context.

8
00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:33.099
For example,
imagine an image of the Mona Lisa.

9
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.900
The image is a key data, which can be
the vector embedding of the image itself.

10
00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:44.144
Other data
such as the title, artist, name, location,

11
00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:48.114
and more can be added to the image
embedded as metadata.

12
00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:51.684
Both the key data
and the accompanying metadata

13
00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:55.255
can be stored in MongoDB
as a single document.

14
00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:58.925
By pairing the vector embedded
with metadata,

15
00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:03.596
the overall image data
becomes more informative and useful.

16
00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:06.566
Let's understand
how metadata can be useful

17
00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:09.903
within LLM applications and RAG design
pattern,

18
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.306
Metadata can be used to add
additional context

19
00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:17.277
to the embedding data
for improved relevance and understanding.

20
00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:21.614
Metadata also improves the relevance
of vector search queries by enabling

21
00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:26.186
filtering and sorting based on data
attributed to the embedding data.

22
00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:31.386
This can reduce the scope of the vector
search query operation or its result.

23
00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:35.762
In order to streamline
the results of vector search operation to

24
00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:39.632
improve relevance, you will use metadata
within filtering stages

25
00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:42.802
compose together
with a vector search stage

26
00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:46.539
in an aggregation pipeline.
You will use MongoDB's

27
00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:49.843
aggregation
pipeline to create composable queries,

28
00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:54.380
which makes it easier to think of
and implement complex queries.

29
00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:57.851
Creating an aggregation
pipeline with filtering stages

30
00:01:57.851 --> 00:02:02.288
enables database operation
to produce more relevant results.

31
00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:07.755
Let's see how. An example of one of
the filtering technique is post-filtering.

32
00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:12.165
This is where a vector search stage
is conducted and the results are reduced

33
00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:15.668
based on certain
criteria are referred to as filter.

34
00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:19.739
Imagine you have a user query
that has certain keywords

35
00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:24.177
such as seaside and restaurant,
but it also contains constraints

36
00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:28.348
such as specified quantities of room
and capacity requirement.

37
00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:30.150
You can examine how a post

38
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.354
filtering process will occur
by first starting with the full data set,

39
00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:38.291
then applying a vector
search operation on the full data

40
00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:42.829
set to get results that are semantically
similar to the user query.

41
00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:46.699
Then, in a post-filtering operation,
you apply the filter stage

42
00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:49.736
after the vector search stage
to further reduce

43
00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:53.273
the return result
based on a specified criteria.

44
00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:58.806
Now, let's see another filtering technique
known as pre-filtering.

45
00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:03.483
Using pre-filtering technique
within vector search can produce

